<article class="env-remark" id="0FIX">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FIX">Remark <span data-tag="0FIX">52.26.3</span>.</a> In SGA2 we find the following result. Let $(A, \mathfrak m)$ be a Noetherian local ring. Let $f \in \mathfrak m$. Assume $A$ is a quotient of a regular ring, the element $f$ is a nonzerodivisor, and </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="(a)"><p>
if $\mathfrak p \subset A$ is a prime ideal with $\dim (A/\mathfrak p) = 1$, then $\text{depth}(A_\mathfrak p) \geq 2$, and 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(b)"><p>
$\text{depth}(A/fA) \geq 3$, or equivalently $\text{depth}(A) \geq 4$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Let $U$, resp. $U_0$ be the punctured spectrum of $A$, resp. $A/fA$. Then the map </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (U) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}\nolimits (U_0)  \]
</div>
  <p> is injective. This is <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">Exposee XI, Lemma 3.16</span>, <a href="/bibliography/SGA2">SGA2</a>]</span><a class="footnotemark" href="#a0001120281" id="a0001120281-mark"><sup>1</sup></a>. This result from SGA2 follows from Proposition <a href="/tag/0F2B" data-tag="0F2B">52.26.2</a> because </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
a quotient of a regular ring has a dualizing complex (see Dualizing Complexes, Lemma <a href="/tag/0AWX" data-tag="0AWX">47.21.3</a> and Proposition <a href="/tag/0A7K" data-tag="0A7K">47.15.11</a>), and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
if $\text{depth}(A) \geq 4$ then $\text{depth}(A_\mathfrak p) \geq 2$ for all primes $\mathfrak p$ with $\dim (A/\mathfrak p) = 2$, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0FCC" data-tag="0FCC">10.72.10</a>. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>